why 1 0 RANGE The bass leaps up to C♯ for I⁶ and steps down C♯–B–A: a brief high point at the start of the phrase.
why 3 0 SIMILAR_MOTION A cadence where every resolution falls: soprano B–A, the alto's G♯ drops to E, the seventh D to C♯ and the bass to A.
why 5 0 HARMONIC_RHYTHM The dominant arrives on the last beat and carries into the next bar while the tune leaps up to E. The bass's neighbor note A decorates it; the harmony is simply held.
why 5 1/4 LEAPS The alto climbs D♯–E–F♯–G♯–A by step. A scale line hides the tritone between its ends.
why 7 1/4 DOUBLING Soprano (B–C♯) and tenor (D–C♯) meet on C♯ by step from opposite sides.
why 9 0 DOUBLING Soprano (C♯–B) and alto (A♯–B) meet on B by step; the alto's A♯ is a leading tone and must rise. Bach then surprises with a G major chord under it.
why 11 0 UNRESOLVED_NCT The alto holds B over the A chord as a suspension and then leaps down to E instead of falling to A. Bach lets it go by a leap in this busy final bar; in exercises, resolve it by step.
why 11 1/8 DOUBLING The bass walks down A–G♯–F♯–E–D toward the final cadence, and the chord over its G♯ is a passing one. Its doubled C♯ follows the soprano and tenor.
why 11 3/8 DOUBLING Still the bass's walk down: the A chord over its E is a passing ⁶₄, not the cadence.
why 12 0 SIMILAR_MOTION The final cadence: every voice falls into the A chord, the bass from E to a low A.
